parliament in sri lanka has begun the  process of choosing a successor to  deposed president gorabaya rajapaksa the  chamber will be selecting a new leader  to serve the rest of russia pax's term  after he fled the country on wednesday  following mass protests  the next president will urgently need to  tackle sri lanka's economic crisis  people are struggling with shortages of  many basic necessities like food fuel  medicine and fertilizer  well dw's my nero chowdhury is in  colombo forest hi mineral good to see  you so a new president should be  appointed next week how will they be  chosen  well pablo the process has begun the  parliament has convened today in fact it  is uh in progress right now as we speak  now the plan of action is that on july  20th a new president will be chosen  through a secret ballot both votes will  be cast by the parliamentarians through  a secret ballot at the moment what we  know are that there are three names in  the in the fray rana vikrama singha the  current prime minister and the acting  president has thrown his hat in the ring  and though he may have a little edge in  the secret ballot but uh it'll be  interesting to see what happens because  the public mood is still overwhelmingly  against him another name in the fray is  that that of dallas allah perumi who is  a member of the rajapaksa political  party and the third name is that of  sajid premadasa who is the leader of the  principal opposition party  maniro tell us what the new president  will have to do to restore confidence in  the political system in sri lanka  well yes the new president will have a  huge challenge in front of him because  people definitely uh have lost faith in  the current administration so he has he  will have a task cut out for himself  people will keenly look at what calls  will be taken in the first few days of  the person taking over the presidentship  one big demand since the beginning of  the protest has been the abolishment of  executive presidency which is a system  here in sri lanka which gives  overwhelming enormous powers to the head  of the state to the president so that  has been one demand and that has been  the demand of the protesters that  whoever takes over now  should be doing away with this system  minera it's been an intense week to say  the least in sri lanka  what if the past seven days meant for  the country's citizens  uh well july the ninth when people  stormed over and occupied the  presidential palace and presidential  secretariat that you see behind me was a  historic day for the people here and on  july the 13th they stormed and occupied  the prime minister's office as well  giving a clear message that they want  ranul vikrama singha also to step down  as the acting president and the current  prime minister so all these days clearly  were culmination of what people say was  their anger their frustration at the at  the administration at what they say the  fake the false promises that they have  been making they say that the occupation  of these buildings was in a way of show  of strength by these people by the  protesters who call this moment a publix  a people's movement a public uprising  and a symbol of what power the the  people hold in a working or should hold  in a working democracy so for them these  seven days have been reflecting that and  also they say that they they did get a  massive victory because after that day  got away pakistan had to flee and had to  ultimately resign which was their  biggest demand since the beginning  manira thanks for your reporting dw's  manira chowdhury in colombo  well for more on that we're joined by  ambika  sat kuna natan a human rights lawyer and  former commissioner of the human rights  commission of sri lanka welcome to dw so  for you as a human rights lawyer what  would be the most urgent challenges for  a new president to tackle  uh well i think before we get to the  challenges it is important that the  parliament elects the person who has  the public trust and also legitimacy  right now it appears that the person uh  who might be elected is the current  acting president ronald vikrama singh  who did not even win his seat to  parliament and against whose premiership  there has been a lot of opposition so i  think public trust and legitimacy is key  if the new president is to go ahead with  economic recovery because political  stability is important and we're not  going to get that if the new president  doesn't have public support that said  three things the new president would  have to do and to take forward is  abolition of the executive presidency  the law that allows them to call out the  armed forces to maintain law and order  that would have to be rescinded and not  reissued and finally elections a very  clear plan to hold elections as soon as  practically possible is the way out of  this impasse  so  how would people actually react if the  interim presidents or the former prime  minister actually um  goes into power  i think the public are already very  concerned about it you can see that in  the protest you can see that in social  media and if he is elected that would uh  lead to the protests continuing that  would lead to uh tensions it would lead  to more conflict and a lot of public  opposition which would create or  exacerbate the existing instability  which means we really can't focus on the  important issues like solving the  economic crisis and dealing with the  basic needs of the people who have been  subjected to immense suffering  let's talk about the former president  now  he and his family have been blamed for  mismanaging the country there there have  been allegations of war crimes and  corruption  would it not have been better if the  president didn't flee the country  well uh  if the president my personal opinion is  if the president had remained in the  country  that would have created more instability  as it is i think the presence of the  rajapaksa's continued presence means  continued instability because they are a  very effective opposition and their aim  is to get back into power plus the  history of the criminal justice system  in sri lanka does not inspire confidence  particularly in relation to holding  those responsible for human rights  violations accountable so perhaps since  universal jurisdiction is widely uh  widely used now increasingly used there  is perhaps a better chance of holding  him accountable outside the country than  inside  ambika sadkuna natan former commissioner  of the human rights commission of sri  lanka and a human rights lawyer thanks  for joining us on dw  thank you
 

